00:13:04,259 --> 00:13:06,259
and also industrial design.

175
00:13:06,259 --> 00:13:11,259
So I'll be talking about two main research threads that we've pursued

176
00:13:11,259 --> 00:13:16,259
and ways in which we've benefited from CLO3D.

177
00:13:16,259 --> 00:13:22,259
I thought it was a stroke of genius that Catherine came up with a title for our presentation,

178
00:13:22,259 --> 00:13:29,259
and that unstitched is not only a catchall for a lot of our visual output,

179
00:13:29,259 --> 00:13:30,259
but often functions as the inspiration for our work.

180
00:13:30,259 --> 00:13:36,259
And the motivation for both the chair that you see,

181
00:13:36,259 --> 00:13:39,259
which is a self-shaping textile,

182
00:13:39,259 --> 00:13:40,259
and the shoe,

183
00:13:40,259 --> 00:13:42,259
which is a 3D woven textile,

184
00:13:42,259 --> 00:13:45,259
was really to push what a textile could be.

185
00:13:45,259 --> 00:13:46,259
And in fact,

186
00:13:46,259 --> 00:13:50,259
each comes off the loom as a single textile,

187
00:13:50,259 --> 00:13:53,259
and with minimal finishing and certainly no stitching,

188
00:13:53,259 --> 00:13:58,259
it becomes the designed woven object.

189
00:13:58,259 --> 00:14:05,259
So the first thread started at the height of the pandemic.

190
00:14:05,259 --> 00:14:10,259
So as a way to revive ourselves as a research group,

191
00:14:10,259 --> 00:14:12,259
after the deadening effects of lockdown,

192
00:14:12,259 --> 00:14:16,259
we were creating these complex woven fabrics,

193
00:14:16,259 --> 00:14:18,259
complex both in structure,

194
00:14:18,259 --> 00:14:20,259
as well as sensorily,

195
00:14:20,259 --> 00:14:23,259
in that they invited complex tactile interaction.

196
00:14:23,259 --> 00:14:26,259
So these woven textiles all have some context,

197
00:14:26,259 --> 00:14:28,259
of active yarn.

198
00:14:28,259 --> 00:14:31,259
So a material that strongly tends towards shrinkage,

199
00:14:31,259 --> 00:14:33,259
like elastomeric,

200
00:14:33,259 --> 00:14:34,259
even wool,

201
00:14:34,259 --> 00:14:35,259
or stiffness,

202
00:14:35,259 --> 00:14:36,259
like linen,

203
00:14:36,259 --> 00:14:38,259
or other physical behavior.

204
00:14:38,259 --> 00:14:42,259
So most of these come off the loom still in this rectangular form,

205
00:14:42,259 --> 00:14:46,259
but when activated by heat or moisture will take on these complex forms.

206
00:14:46,259 --> 00:14:51,259
And the fundamental question in the field of self-shaping or active material

207
00:14:51,259 --> 00:14:52,259
is,

208
00:14:52,259 --> 00:14:54,259
how do we create a material that is more flexible,

209
00:14:54,259 --> 00:14:55,259
more flexible,

210
00:14:55,259 --> 00:14:57,259
is how do we,

211
00:14:57,259 --> 00:14:58,259
as designers,

212
00:14:58,259 --> 00:15:02,259
can harness these forces and get specific outcomes.

213
00:15:02,259 --> 00:15:04,259
So as many of you know,

214
00:15:04,259 --> 00:15:08,259
weaving these fabrics can be very time,

215
00:15:08,259 --> 00:15:10,259
material and labor intensive.

216
00:15:10,259 --> 00:15:14,259
So we wanted to understand what the fabric behavior would be

217
00:15:14,259 --> 00:15:18,259
in a visually intuitive way before actually making them.

218
00:15:18,259 --> 00:15:23,259
So our first cut was to use grasshopper for rhino,

219
00:15:23,259 --> 00:15:25,259
which was a tool that we were,

220
00:15:25,259 --> 00:15:31,740
familiar with within the institution, to sketch examples of self-shaping fabrics

221
00:15:31,740 --> 00:15:35,419
as 3D modeled surfaces with shrinking and stiffness regions.

222
00:15:39,500 --> 00:15:45,740
But this was also our first foray into CLO3D, trying to achieve the kind of behavior we were

223
00:15:45,740 --> 00:15:51,740
seeing from trial and error physical sampling, like boundary distortion, crumpling, pleating,

224
00:15:51,740 --> 00:15:58,220
and bubbling. And here we used really seamed pattern pieces as a stand-in for

225
00:15:58,220 --> 00:16:02,220
specifying regions with different shrinking and stiffness rates.

226
00:16:04,700 --> 00:16:09,740
And because these textiles undergo significant surface change from its rectangular state,

227
00:16:09,740 --> 00:16:15,340
it was extremely useful to be able to visualize this shape change in applications such as

228
00:16:15,340 --> 00:16:21,659
on a chair frame. And with the added confidence of these visualizations,

229
00:16:21,740 --> 00:16:27,340
and simulation tools, we could then scale to full scale prototyping, confident that the shrinkage

230
00:16:27,340 --> 00:16:33,659
when heated would result in a good fit for the prefabricated chair frame, and that the localized

231
00:16:33,659 --> 00:16:41,259
textures would serve their intended purpose. And we were interested in practical applications

232
00:16:41,259 --> 00:16:46,460
of these textiles, but also curious about what we could learn from just observing and interacting

233
00:16:46,460 --> 00:16:51,659
with them. So much of the information we gather about an object,

234
00:16:51,740 --> 00:16:57,659
its weight, stiffness, smoothness, comes from sensing how it changes when we make these slight

235
00:16:57,659 --> 00:17:03,820
hand movements. If I rest on this fabric, will it flatten under my weight or spring back when I get

236
00:17:03,820 --> 00:17:09,980
up? We're interested in not just the final form of the textile after the shape change has taken

237
00:17:09,980 --> 00:17:16,539
place, but all of these additional possible states. And the sensory experience is both about

238
00:17:16,539 --> 00:17:21,660
the behavior and the application. Moreover, seeing the textile form into a texture,

239
00:17:21,660 --> 00:17:29,660
its final relaxed state, as we know from seeing simulation of any textile in CLO, contributes to

240
00:17:29,660 --> 00:17:37,580
the sensory experience. So since our textiles had these very pronounced surface textures,

241
00:17:37,580 --> 00:17:43,259
communicating these sensory qualities in a virtual environment without feel also naturally brought us

242
00:17:43,259 --> 00:17:51,100
to the world of digital materials. Designing digital materials, PBRs that are used often within CLO,

243
00:17:51,099 --> 00:17:59,339
is typically done either procedurally or using some kind of scanning. And presenting this work

244
00:17:59,339 --> 00:18:05,339
at the preeminent computer graphics conference SIGGRAPH in 2022, we were introduced to a new

245
00:18:05,339 --> 00:18:13,179
digital material capture solution brewing between HP and Adobe substance called HPZ Captus. And this

246
00:18:13,179 --> 00:18:20,139
was a full circle moment for us as a research group, which really had its origins from one of

247
00:18:20,140 --> 00:18:29,660
our founders watching Brave back in 2012 and thinking, if the animators make these make-believe

248
00:18:29,660 --> 00:18:36,860
fabric look so convincing, surely these tools can benefit textile designers in their design process.

249
00:18:40,540 --> 00:18:45,100
And we were amongst the first to be able to use the Captus. This is, again,

250
00:18:45,100 --> 00:18:49,180
this digital material capture device. In its early stages,

251
00:18:49,180 --> 00:18:56,539
this is a collaboration between HP and Adobe, and it uses the hardware of the photometric scanning

252
00:18:56,539 --> 00:19:03,180
from HP integrated with the software of Adobe Substance Sampler. And going from an existing

253
00:19:03,180 --> 00:19:11,259
fabric sample, like this lacy knit swatch, to packaging it in a usable format in CLO took me

254
00:19:11,259 --> 00:19:17,420
about 10 minutes. And right now, it can't capture all of the material maps, but already we see the

255
00:19:17,420 --> 00:19:18,700
potential for having digital materials that are able to capture all of the material maps. And so,

256
00:19:18,700 --> 00:19:22,299
we've been able to capture all of the digital materials with much richer tactile properties built in.

257
00:19:28,779 --> 00:19:34,220
So, this was also the central ambition for this project, in which we developed a collection of

258
00:19:34,220 --> 00:19:39,420
novel materials with highly specific sensory qualities that exist both digitally and

259
00:19:39,420 --> 00:19:44,539
physically. Notice this wasn't just digital twinning, but instead balancing realistic

260
00:19:44,539 --> 00:19:46,940
qualities with the imaginary and the emotional.

261
00:19:49,660 --> 00:19:56,299
The next thread was work with the RISD museum's department of costume and textiles,

262
00:19:56,299 --> 00:19:59,980
and the museum textiles setting provided this ready-made testbed for

263
00:20:00,779 --> 00:20:05,980
the previous project's work, given that so much of the emotional draw of textiles and garments

264
00:20:05,980 --> 00:20:11,980
involves experience of wearing and touching. And the primary goal that we set out was to explore

265
00:20:11,980 --> 00:20:18,140
ways we might employ digital materials to enhance visitors' tactile understanding and sensory

266
00:20:18,140 --> 00:20:23,820
experience of garments in a museum gallery setting, especially those garments that cannot,

267
00:20:23,820 --> 00:20:31,100
for one reason or another, be shown on bodily form. And this project made central use of Clo3D,

268
00:20:31,100 --> 00:20:36,540
and I'm thankful for the contributions of Catherine, as well as Jim and Lindsay LeFoune

269
00:20:36,540 --> 00:20:45,740
in the Clo New York team. So for this project, we specifically selected South Asian garments

270
00:20:45,740 --> 00:20:51,579
as our focus, and all of these pieces we chose are unstitched garments, in that they are both

271
00:20:51,579 --> 00:20:57,259
fabric lengths as well as garments that derive their volumetric form through wrapping or draping

272
00:20:57,259 --> 00:21:05,099
around the body. And like the textiles that you see here displayed on the walls of this

273
00:21:05,099 --> 00:21:10,299
recently mounted exhibition, the textiles that we selected would also be shown flat,

274
00:21:10,299 --> 00:21:15,660
so as not to stress these fragile textiles. All of these textiles we chose were

275
00:21:16,299 --> 00:21:21,980
over 100 years old, and yet these textiles really come to life, as we know, as garments through

276
00:21:21,980 --> 00:21:28,460
folding, pleating, and tucking, which often is a leap too far for many museum goers' imagination

277
00:21:28,460 --> 00:21:37,819
if they were displayed flat on the wall. So this conversation with the museum started shortly

278
00:21:37,819 --> 00:21:43,660
before the Clo accreditation course that I was lucky enough to attend in August of 2023,

279
00:21:43,660 --> 00:21:45,579
and was able to end that week with a good

280
00:21:45,740 --> 00:21:52,940
sketch of one sari drape. And thanks to Jim and Lindsey, that sketch was refined significantly

281
00:21:52,940 --> 00:21:58,940
and became the proof of concept for this project. So the sari has many different drapes, but a

282
00:21:58,940 --> 00:22:04,460
relatively small number of operations. And the strategy that I have found most effective is to

283
00:22:04,460 --> 00:22:10,059
break up the fabric length, where the standard length of a sari is between five and a half

284
00:22:10,059 --> 00:22:15,579
meters to as long as eight meters long, into smaller lengths devoted to one layer,

285
00:22:15,740 --> 00:22:21,500
and then to seam them together. The translation for most of these operations

286
00:22:22,140 --> 00:22:27,740
is fairly straightforward, with a couple of exceptions, like knotting and tucking, which I

287
00:22:27,740 --> 00:22:36,140
found of course possible to do within Clo, but it looked better with tacking or more securely sewing.

288
00:22:39,099 --> 00:22:45,660
And these translations, while good enough for rendering, would require more adjustments on

289
00:22:45,660 --> 00:22:53,340
a completely different approach if we were to get a good animation. The result depicted in these

290
00:22:53,340 --> 00:23:00,300
stills shows the gap that still exists between rendering and animation. The substantial amount

291
00:23:00,300 --> 00:23:05,900
of fabric wrapped around the body created this heavy model with multiple layers. And while the

292
00:23:05,900 --> 00:23:11,019
simulated cloth properties could be adjusted to correct some of the bounce, the primary issue lay

293
00:23:11,019 --> 00:23:15,580
with the motion file used. You can see that the large stride of the model was still a little bit

294
00:23:15,579 --> 00:23:19,579
loose andnee in its overall design. As a result, the animation's movement wasairs might not be

295
00:23:19,579 --> 00:23:40,019
as natural as when you imagine a simple, like a

296
00:23:40,019 --> 00:23:41,019
demonstration of a fabric spring over a Ethereumroid.

297
00:23:41,019 --> 00:23:41,980
In the metadata of the model we see that, while silhouettes may not be always typical to one

298
00:23:41,980 --> 00:23:43,019
walking a runway, maybe not the everyday movements one might have while wearing a sari.

299
00:23:43,019 --> 00:23:45,019
And this is kind of an internal look. Notice here that this is what you see from a museum database,

300
00:23:45,019 --> 00:23:45,419
and that both in the display as well as the descriptions, none of the distinctive sensory properties that we chose,

301
00:23:45,579 --> 00:23:49,419
the fabrics for are really reflected in these.

302
00:23:50,379 --> 00:23:55,220
And it's probably the rare exhibition on textiles

303
00:23:55,220 --> 00:23:58,579
that can really communicate how a textile feels.

304
00:23:58,579 --> 00:24:01,939
And increasingly as AI generation depends so much

305
00:24:01,939 --> 00:24:04,619
on textual and image description,

306
00:24:04,619 --> 00:24:06,539
or rather the richness of that,

307
00:24:06,539 --> 00:24:09,419
the ability to get the sensory description right

308
00:24:09,419 --> 00:24:11,899
on the database level seems more pressing.

309
00:24:11,900 --> 00:24:16,900
So we wanted to create a sensory rich database

310
00:24:18,060 --> 00:24:20,860
that showcases the breadth of these textiles tradition.

311
00:24:20,860 --> 00:24:23,700
So for each of the selected fabrics,

312
00:24:23,700 --> 00:24:26,180
we created representations of them,

313
00:24:26,180 --> 00:24:29,420
again, using Adobe Substance 3D.

314
00:24:29,420 --> 00:24:33,300
We modeled these 3D assets in application

315
00:24:33,300 --> 00:24:37,300
and furnished a description that alludes

316
00:24:37,300 --> 00:24:39,740
to the sensory experience of interacting

317
00:24:39,740 --> 00:24:41,060
with these textiles.

318
00:24:41,900 --> 00:24:45,980
And as many of us know,

319
00:24:45,980 --> 00:24:48,259
so much of the sensory experience also comes

320
00:24:48,259 --> 00:24:51,060
from understanding the process.

321
00:24:51,060 --> 00:24:54,060
Looking at the back of this textile,

322
00:24:54,060 --> 00:24:55,860
this is called a bag fulcari,

323
00:24:55,860 --> 00:25:00,860
which is a nearly extinct extraordinary embroidery technique

324
00:25:03,340 --> 00:25:06,780
from Punjab area where the back of the fabric

325
00:25:06,780 --> 00:25:09,140
not only is beautiful in itself,

326
00:25:09,140 --> 00:25:11,060
but tells us so much about the process of making it.

327
00:25:11,900 --> 00:25:12,980
And as we move forward,

328
00:25:12,980 --> 00:25:15,340
we can see that the fabric that is being used

329
00:25:15,340 --> 00:25:18,540
is not only designed to be used as a kind of display,

330
00:25:18,540 --> 00:25:19,860
but also as a kind of display for the user

331
00:25:19,860 --> 00:25:22,220
to see what the fabric is doing as well.

332
00:25:22,220 --> 00:25:23,460
And using digital materials,

333
00:25:23,460 --> 00:25:25,740
we could showcase both sides of the fabrics,

334
00:25:25,740 --> 00:25:28,660
which is not easily done in a display behind glass.

335
00:25:31,660 --> 00:25:35,820
And substance materials or PBR materials in general,

336
00:25:35,820 --> 00:25:38,580
but substance materials can be easily brought into CLO.

337
00:25:38,580 --> 00:25:40,060
And for the ones that we design procedurally,

338
00:25:40,059 --> 00:25:46,419
Amongst the South Asian collection, we had some extraordinary fabrics made using the

339
00:25:46,419 --> 00:25:48,039
bandhani technique.

340
00:25:48,039 --> 00:25:53,859
This is commonly practiced in the western Indian states of Gujarat and Rajasthan.

341
00:25:53,859 --> 00:26:00,579
In this process, parts of the textiles are tied into knots with yarn to resist the absorption

342
00:26:00,579 --> 00:26:04,539
of the dye, resulting in these spotted patterns.

343
00:26:04,539 --> 00:26:10,299
This finished fabric is highly textured and, in this case, very lightweight and vibrantly

344
00:26:10,299 --> 00:26:14,659
colored.

345
00:26:14,659 --> 00:26:18,220
But modeling the feel of bandhani was challenging.

346
00:26:18,220 --> 00:26:22,539
The attempts to create a digital material were not so successful, it really came in

347
00:26:22,539 --> 00:26:28,779
looking quite flat, possibly because the knots are so dense that what gets picked up is a

348
00:26:28,779 --> 00:26:34,019
uniform height, which resulted in a digital material that looks thick and monolithic.

349
00:26:34,019 --> 00:26:40,119
So what seemed to work better was to generate the pattern of knots, in this case, just using

350
00:26:40,119 --> 00:26:48,980
a circle packing technique in Rhino and Brasshopper, and then to apply elastic for the knot circles.

351
00:26:48,980 --> 00:26:53,639
It did become a very heavy model, but Catherine was able to get it to render.

352
00:26:53,639 --> 00:26:58,720
And you can see that what came out really came out as looking quite light.

353
00:26:58,720 --> 00:27:02,139
I think it reflects the lightweight feel of the fabric.

354
00:27:02,139 --> 00:27:03,819
Thank you.

355
00:27:04,019 --> 00:27:10,019
And this work culminated in an exhibition at the RISD Museum called Sensory Silhouettes,

356
00:27:10,019 --> 00:27:17,480
which just ended its six-month run not too long ago.

357
00:27:17,480 --> 00:27:22,920
But thanks to the technical support I reached from Cloth3D for my research, I very recently

358
00:27:22,920 --> 00:27:28,940
started using Cloth3D as a teaching tool and will be happy to report on how that has gone

359
00:27:28,940 --> 00:27:32,019
at the end of this school year.

360
00:27:32,019 --> 00:27:33,019
Thank you very much.

361
00:27:34,019 --> 00:27:35,180
You're very welcome.

